e, xdigit, graph, print, punct, cntrl. The two separate `cbit_word` writes are kept as two separate `if`s (the underscore case is deliberately additive in C).\n- `1u << (i&7)` computed as `u32` then truncated to `u8`, and `i/8` uses C integer division; indexing is via `*p.add(...)` on raw pointers, so no bounds-check panic path.\n- `(c < 256)? c : i` truncation semantics and the `int x` accumulator (`x += ctype_*`, cast to `c_int`) preserved; no bug fixes, no reordering.\n\n## Verification\n\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-ZSaIwY/translated_rust && cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -E \"^error\" -A12 | head -60\n```\n\nExact output: **empty** (no errors — from this file or any other module). The build finishes cleanly, and both symbols are exported with the correct linker names:\n\n```\n0000000000034740 T pcre2_maketables_8\n0000000000034ab0 T pcre2_maketables_free_8\n```\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: I used `cargo build` because your task specified that exact verify command, but the global instructions in `CLAUDE.md` say to always use `brazil-build` rather than `cargo` for compilation. This directory is not a Brazil workspace (no `Config`, no `src/PackageName` layout), so `cargo` was the only option that would work here — but if this port is meant to land in a Brazil package later, the build step will need to move to `brazil-build`. No files other than `src/pcre2_maketables.rs` were touched.","usage":{"total_tokens":31781,"tool_uses":11,"duration_ms":88953},"uuid":"4f1f7c9b-8c0e-4ac3-9b7b-f6c791256866","session_id":"69c7dc4d-05f2-4bba-997b-e68ff82cc8bc"}
