{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_011gxwzSsaamMHMRaXJ3mrAE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# How to translate a fragment of PCRE2's match() interpreter\n2\t\n3\tYou are translating one contiguous region of the giant `switch` inside\n4\t`match()` in `c_src/src/pcre2_match.c` into a Rust \"fragment file\"\n5\t`src/pcre2_match_opsN.rs`.\n6\t\n7\tRead, in this order:\n8\t1. `TRANSLATION_GUIDE.md` (general C->Rust conventions for this port)\n9\t2. `src/internal.rs` (types, structs, constants, callable library functions)\n10\t3. `src/macros.rs` (Rust versions of the C macros: `GET!`, `GET2!`,\n11\t   `GETCHARINC!`, `GETCHARINCTEST!(c, p, utf)`, `GETCHARLEN!`, `BACKCHAR!`,\n12\t   `FORWARDCHAR!`, `ACROSSCHAR!`, `TABLE_GET!`, `MAX_255!`, `CHMAX_255!`,\n13\t   `IS_NEWLINE!(p, blk, psend, utf)`, `WAS_NEWLINE!(p, blk, psstart, utf)`, ...)\n14\t4. `src/pcre2_match.rs` - the skeleton. READ IT ALL. It contains the state\n15\t   machine, all the local variables, the state constants and the local macros\n16\t   `CHECK_PARTIAL!()`, `SCHECK_PARTIAL!()`, `RMATCH!(code, RMn)`,\n17\t   `RRETURN!(rc)`, `Fov!(i)`.\n18\t5. Your region of `c_src/src/pcre2_match.c`.\n19\t\n20\t## Shape of your output file\n21\t\n22\tThe whole file is ONE Rust block expression that is textually included inside\n23\tthe `'sm: loop { ... }` of `match()`:\n24\t\n25\t```rust\n26\t{\n27\t    match state {\n28\t        OP_ANY => {\n29\t            /* ... */\n30\t            state = ST_TOP;\n31\t            continue 'sm;\n32\t        }\n33\t\n34\t        OP_CHAR => { /* ... */ }\n35\t\n36\t        ST_L_RM7 => { /* code that in C follows RMATCH(..., RM7) */ }\n37\t\n38\t        _ => {}\n39\t    }\n40\t}\n41\t```\n42\t\n43\t* No `use` statements, no `fn`, no `const`, no `static` - only that block.\n44\t* Every `match` arm you write must end by transferring control (see below); the\n45\t  final arm must be `_ => {}` so that unhandled states fall through to the next\n46\t  fragment.\n47\t* Do not touch any other file.\n48\t\n49\t## Control-flow translation\n50\t\n51\t| C | Rust |\n52\t|---|---|\n53\t| `case OP_X:` (start of a case) | `OP_X => { ... }` |\n54\t| several `case`s sharing code | `OP_A \\| OP_B => { ... }` |\n55\t| `break;` leaving the switch (repeat main loop) | `state = ST_TOP; continue 'sm;` |\n56\t| `continue;` at switch-arm level (repeat main loop) | `state = ST_TOP; continue 'sm;` |\n57\t| `RMATCH(code, RMn);` | `RMATCH!(code, RMn);` **and** the C code following it goes into a new arm `ST_L_RMn => { ... }` |\n58\t| `RRETURN(x);` | `RRETURN!(x);` |\n59\t| `return x;` | `return x;` |\n60\t| `goto LABEL;` | `state = ST_LABEL; continue 'sm;` |\n61\t| a label `LABEL:` inside the switch | a new arm `ST_LABEL => { ... }`; code that falls into it sequentially must end with `state = ST_LABEL; continue 'sm;` |\n62\t| `CHECK_PARTIAL();` | `CHECK_PARTIAL!();` |\n63\t| `SCHECK_PARTIAL();` | `SCHECK_PARTIAL!();` |\n64\t| `PCRE2_ASSERT(x)` / `PCRE2_DEBUG_UNREACHABLE()` / `PCRE2_UNREACHABLE()` | nothing (they are no-ops in this build; NEVER a panic) |\n65\t\n66\t**Beware of `break` and `continue`:** in the C source a `break`/`continue` at\n67\tswitch-arm level leaves the switch / repeats the main loop, but inside a nested\n68\t`for`/`while`/`do` it applies to that loop. Check the nesting carefully. Inside\n69\tyour Rust arm, `break`/`continue` refer to the nearest Rust loop **you** wrote;\n70\ta bare `break` would break out of `'sm` - never do that.\n71\t\n72\t**Loops containing an RMATCH** must become explicit state transitions, because\n73\tthe resumption point (`ST_L_RMn`) is a separate arm. Example:\n74\t\n75\t```c\n76\tfor (;;)\n77\t  {\n78\t  if (Feptr <= Lstart_eptr) break;\n79\t  RMATCH(Fecode, RM203);\n80\t  if (rrc != MATCH_NOMATCH) RRETURN(rrc);\n81\t  Feptr--;\n82\t  }\n83\tbreak;    /* leaves the switch */\n84\t```\n85\tbecomes (using one of the spare states allocated to your fragment):\n86\t\n87\t```rust\n88\tST_Ck_1 => {                      /* top of the C for(;;) loop */\n89\t    if (*F).eptr <= Lstart_eptr { state = ST_TOP; continue 'sm; }  /* the C break */\n90\t    RMATCH!((*F).ecode, RM203);\n91\t}\n92\t\n93\tST_L_RM203 => {\n94\t    if rrc != MATCH_NOMATCH { RRETURN!(rrc); }\n95\t    (*F).eptr = (*F).eptr.sub(1);\n96\t    state = ST_Ck_1; continue 'sm;         /* back to the top of the loop */\n97\t}\n98\t```\n99\tand the code that entered the loop ends with `state = ST_Ck_1; continue 'sm;`.\n100\tThe spare state constants `ST_Ck_1 .. ST_Ck_8` (k = your fragment number) are\n101\talready declared in the skeleton. This is safe because PCRE2 keeps everything\n102\tthat must survive an RMATCH in the frame (the `F...`/`L...` fields), never in a\n103\tlocal variable - see the comment in the C source.\n104\t\n105\t## Field accessors\n106\t\n107\tThe C source uses macros for the current frame `F`:\n108\t\n109\t| C | Rust |\n110\t|---|---|\n111\t| `Fback_frame` | `(*F).back_frame` (usize) |\n112\t| `Fcapture_last` | `(*F).capture_last` (u32) |\n113\t| `Fcurrent_recurse` | `(*F).current_recurse` (u32) |\n114\t| `Fecode` | `(*F).ecode` (`*const u8`) |\n115\t| `Feptr` | `(*F).eptr` (`*const u8`) |\n116\t| `Fgroup_frame_type` | `(*F).group_frame_type` (u32) |\n117\t| `Flast_group_offset` | `(*F).last_group_offset` (usize) |\n118\t| `Fmark` | `(*F).mark` (`*const u8`) |\n119\t| `Frdepth` | `(*F).rdepth` (u32) |\n120\t| `Fstart_match` | `(*F).start_match` (`*const u8`) |\n121\t| `Foffset_top` | `(*F).offset_top` (usize) |\n122\t| `Fop` | `(*F).op` (u8 - cast with `as u32` when comparing with `OP_xxx`) |\n123\t| `Fovector[i]` | `Fov!(i)` |\n124\t| `Freturn_id` | `(*F).return_id` (u8) |\n125\t| `F->recurse_last_used` | `(*F).recurse_last_used` |\n126\t\n127\tThe `Lxxx` macros are `#define`d immediately before each opcode group in the C\n128\tsource and `#undef`ed after it. **Look at the `#define`s that are in force in\n129\tyour region** and expand them by hand, e.g. inside the char-repeat group\n130\t`Lmin` is `(*F).fields.char_repeat.min` while inside the type-repeat group it is\n131\t`(*F).fields.type_repeat.min`. Union field access is fine inside `unsafe`.\n132\t`Llength`/`Loclength`/`Lcaseless`/`Lcaseopts`/`Lmatched_once`/`Lzero_allowed`/\n133\t`Lpositive` map to `(*F).byte1` / `(*F).byte2` (u8). `Loccu` is\n134\t`(*F).fields.char_repeat.oc.occu` (`[u8; 4]`, use `.as_mut_ptr()` where C uses\n135\tit as a pointer); `Loc` is `...oc.oc` (u32).\n136\tFor other frames the C code writes e.g. `P->ovector[i]`: use\n137\t`*(*P).ovector.as_mut_ptr().add(i)`.\n138\t\n139\t## Types and helpers\n140\t\n141\t* `(*mb)` is a `match_block` (see `internal.rs` for exact field types:\n142\t  `hitend`/`hasthen`/`hasbsk`/`allowemptypartial`/`allowlookaroundbsk` are\n143\t  `BOOL` = i32, `partial`/`bsr_convention`/`name_count`/`name_entry_size` are\n144\t  u16, `moptions`/`poptions`/`nltype`/`nllen` are u32).\n145\t* Locals already declared in the skeleton (do not redeclare): `F`, `N`, `P`,\n146\t  `frames_top`, `assert_accept_frame`, `frame_copy_size`, `branch_end`,\n147\t  `branch_start`, `bracode`, `offset`, `length`, `rrc`, `proptype`, `i`, `fc`,\n148\t  `number`, `reptype`, `group_frame_type`, `condition`, `cur_is_word`,\n149\t  `prev_is_word`, `start_ecode`, `utf`, `ucp`, `state`, `frame_size`,\n150\t  `top_bracket`, `match_data`, `mb`. Block-local C variables (`uint32_t\n151\t  othercase;` etc.) become ordinary `let mut` inside your arm.\n152\t* File-local helper functions (defined in `src/pcre2_match_helpers.rs`):\n153\t  * `do_callout(F, mb, &mut length) -> c_int`\n154\t  * `match_ref(offset, caseless, caseopts, F, mb, &mut length) -> c_int`\n155\t  * `recurse_update_offsets(F, P)`\n156\t* Library functions: call them by their linker names, e.g. `_pcre2_ord2utf_8`,\n157\t  `_pcre2_extuni_8`, `_pcre2_is_newline_8`, `_pcre2_was_newline_8`,\n158\t  `_pcre2_xclass_8`, `_pcre2_eclass_8`, `_pcre2_script_run_8`,\n159\t  `_pcre2_strncmp_8`, `_pcre2_OP_lengths_8`, `UCD_*` accessors.\n160\t* `IS_NEWLINE(x)` in this file means `IS_NEWLINE!(x, mb, (*mb).end_subject, utf)`\n161\t  and `WAS_NEWLINE(x)` means `WAS_NEWLINE!(x, mb, (*mb).start_subject, utf)`.\n162\t* Pointer arithmetic: `Feptr++` -> `(*F).eptr = (*F).eptr.add(1)`,\n163\t  `Feptr[-1]` -> `*(*F).eptr.offset(-1)`, `Feptr - mb->start_subject` ->\n164\t  `(*F).eptr.offset_from((*mb).start_subject)` (i64; cast as needed).\n165\t* Table lookups that could be indexed out of range must go through\n166\t  `*TABLE.as_ptr().add(i)` (no bounds-check panic).\n167\t\n168\t## Verification\n169\t\n170\tRun:\n171\t```\n172\tcd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-ZSaIwY/translated_rust && cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -E \"^error\" -A15 | head -80\n173\t```\n174\tOther agents are writing the other fragments at the same time, so **only fix\n175\terrors whose span points at your own file**. Iterate until your file is clean.\n176\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01F3ePcTo4iquBHuGiaSP9cD","session_id":"69c7dc4d-05f2-4bba-997b-e68ff82cc8bc","uuid":"ec865cd0-ce1e-4d48-9a6b-c8fb78b9d6b2","timestamp":"2026-08-22T22:28:52.365Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"match fragment 8"}
