{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01RKMYdQteLjaoLShDFhQCF3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# How to translate a fragment of PCRE2's internal_dfa_match()\n2\t\n3\tYou are translating one contiguous region of the giant `switch (codevalue)`\n4\tinside `internal_dfa_match()` in `c_src/src/pcre2_dfa_match.c` into a Rust\n5\t\"fragment file\" `src/pcre2_dfa_opsN.rs`.\n6\t\n7\tRead, in this order:\n8\t1. `TRANSLATION_GUIDE.md` (general C->Rust conventions for this port)\n9\t2. `src/internal.rs` (types, structs, constants, callable library functions)\n10\t3. `src/macros.rs` (Rust versions of the C macros)\n11\t4. `src/pcre2_dfa_match.rs` (the skeleton: the prologue, the subject loop, the\n12\t   active-state loop, the local macros) and `src/pcre2_dfa_match_head.rs`\n13\t   (constants, `stateblock`, `RWS_anchor`, and the `OPX_*` constants).\n14\t5. Your region of `c_src/src/pcre2_dfa_match.c`.\n15\t\n16\t## Shape of your output file\n17\t\n18\tThe whole file is ONE Rust block expression that is textually included inside\n19\tthe `'next_active_state: { ... }` block of the active-state loop:\n20\t\n21\t```rust\n22\t{\n23\t    match codevalue {\n24\t        OP_KET | OP_KETRMIN | OP_KETRMAX | OP_KETRPOS => {\n25\t            /* ... */\n26\t            break 'next_active_state;\n27\t        }\n28\t\n29\t        OPX_PROP_TYPEPLUS | OPX_PROP_TYPEMINPLUS | OPX_PROP_TYPEPOSPLUS => { /* ... */ }\n30\t\n31\t        _ => {}\n32\t    }\n33\t}\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\t* No `use`, no `fn`, no `const`, no `static` - only that block.\n37\t* The last arm must be `_ => {}` so unhandled opcodes fall through to the next\n38\t  fragment (and finally to the C `default: return PCRE2_ERROR_DFA_UITEM;`).\n39\t* Rust patterns cannot contain arithmetic: the C case labels\n40\t  `OP_PROP_EXTRA + OP_TYPEPLUS` etc. have named constants `OPX_PROP_TYPEPLUS`,\n41\t  `OPX_EXTUNI_TYPEQUERY`, `OPX_ANYNL_TYPESTAR`, `OPX_HSPACE_TYPEEXACT`,\n42\t  `OPX_VSPACE_TYPEPOSUPTO`, ... (pattern: `OPX_<PROP|EXTUNI|ANYNL|HSPACE|VSPACE>_<TYPExxx>`)\n43\t  declared in `src/pcre2_dfa_match_head.rs`.\n44\t\n45\t## Control flow\n46\t\n47\t| C | Rust |\n48\t|---|---|\n49\t| `break;` that leaves the `switch(codevalue)` | `break 'next_active_state;` |\n50\t| `continue;` at switch-arm level (next active state) | `break 'next_active_state;` |\n51\t| `goto NEXT_ACTIVE_STATE;` | `break 'next_active_state;` |\n52\t| `return x;` | `return x;` |\n53\t| `ADD_ACTIVE(x,y)` / `ADD_ACTIVE_DATA(x,y,z)` / `ADD_NEW(x,y)` / `ADD_NEW_DATA(x,y,z)` | `ADD_ACTIVE!(x, y)` / `ADD_ACTIVE_DATA!(x, y, z)` / `ADD_NEW!(x, y)` / `ADD_NEW_DATA!(x, y, z)` (they can `return PCRE2_ERROR_DFA_WSSIZE`, exactly like the C macros) |\n54\t| `PCRE2_ASSERT(...)` / `PCRE2_UNREACHABLE()` | nothing (no-ops; never a panic) |\n55\t\n56\tBeware: `break`/`continue` inside a nested `for`/`while` in the C code refer to\n57\tthat loop; only at `switch`-arm level do they mean \"next active state\".\n58\tThe forward gotos `goto ANYNL01/ANYNL02/ANYNL03` and `goto QS1..QS5` jump into\n59\tthe *following* case's code. Reproduce them faithfully: if the target label is\n60\tinside your own region, restructure with a Rust labelled block or by duplicating\n61\tthe small piece of code the label introduces - but do not change behaviour. (The\n62\tregion boundaries were chosen so that every goto and its target are in the same\n63\tfragment.)\n64\t\n65\t## Available locals (declared by the skeleton - do not redeclare)\n66\t\n67\tFunction level: `mb`, `this_start_code`, `current_subject`, `start_offset`,\n68\t`offsets`, `offsetcount`, `workspace`, `wscount`, `rlevel`, `RWS`,\n69\t`active_states`, `new_states`, `next_active_state`, `next_new_state`, `ctypes`,\n70\t`lcc`, `fcc`, `ptr`, `end_code`, `new_recursive`, `active_count`, `new_count`,\n71\t`match_count`, `start_subject`, `end_subject`, `start_code`, `utf`,\n72\t`utf_or_ucp`, `reset_could_continue`.\n73\tSubject-loop level: `i`, `j`, `clen`, `dlen`, `c`, `d`,