{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a72cd50d3a4f88461","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HJiBMP2uZFfsXJcqRDWdM3","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-ZSaIwY/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-translate-ZSaIwY-translated-rust/69c7dc4d-05f2-4bba-997b-e68ff82cc8bc/tasks/a72cd50d3a4f88461.output","summary":"Fragment 10 is written and verified.\n\n## What was done\n\nWrote `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-ZSaIwY/translated_rust/src/pcre2_match_ops10.rs`, translating `c_src/src/pcre2_match.c` lines 5707–5988 (OP_RECURSE, positive assertions, negative assertions, OP_ASSERT_SCS).\n\n### `match` arms produced (11)\n\n| Arm | C origin |\n|---|---|\n| `OP_RECURSE` | `case OP_RECURSE:` head: `bracode`/`number`, recursion loop-check over `Flast_group_offset`, sets `F->recurse_last_used`, `Lstart_branch`, `Lframe_type` |\n| `ST_C10_1` | top of the OP_RECURSE `for(;;)`: `group_frame_type = Lframe_type; RMATCH(..., RM11)` |\n| `ST_L_RM11` | body after RMATCH: `next_ecode`, backtracking-verb range test, `Lstart_branch = next_ecode`, loops back to `ST_C10_1` |\n| `OP_ASSERT \\| OP_ASSERTBACK \\| OP_ASSERT_NA \\| OP_ASSERTBACK_NA` | enters `ST_C10_2` |\n| `ST_C10_2` | top of the positive-assertion `for(;;)`: `RMATCH(..., RM3)` |\n| `ST_L_RM3` | MATCH_ACCEPT (ovector memcpy, offset_top, mark) + inlined `do/while` tail + `Fecode += 1+LINK_SIZE` |\n| `OP_ASSERT_NOT \\| OP_ASSERTBACK_NOT` | enters `ST_C10_3` |\n| `ST_C10_3` | top of the negative-assertion `for(;;)`: `RMATCH(..., RM4)` |\n| `ST_L_RM4` | the inner `switch(rrc)` as an `if/else if` chain in the same case order |\n| `ST_ASSERT_NOT_FAILED` | the `ASSERT_NOT_FAILED:` label |\n| `OP_ASSERT_SCS` | `length=0`, CREF/DNCREF scan, skip-remaining-options, frame saves, `Feptr`/`mb->end_subject`/`true_end_subject`/`moptions` updates, then the loop top `RMATCH(..., RM38)` |\n| `ST_L_RM38` | MATCH_ACCEPT restore + tail (`Feptr = Lsaved_eptr`), the two error/NOMATCH restore paths, `length = 0` and re-entry to the loop top |\n\nSpare states used: `ST_C10_1`, `ST_C10_2`, `ST_C10_3` (4–8 unused).\n\n### Two deliberate structural deviations (semantics identical, forced by Rust)\n\n1. **`SCS_OFFSET_FOUND` is a labelled `loop` inside the `OP_ASSERT_SCS` arm, not the `ST_SCS_OFFSET_FOUND` arm.** The C label sits *inside* the block that owns the local `PCRE2_SPTR ecode`, and the code after the label needs both `ecode` and the function-local `offset`. Crossing a state boundary would (a) lose `ecode` and (b) hit `error[E0381]: used binding 'offset' is possibly-uninitialized`, because the skeleton declares `let mut offset: PCRE2_SIZE;` without an initializer and Rust's definite-init analysis is not path-sensitive across the `'sm` loop head. I verified E0381 fires for exactly this shape. The single labelled `loop` reproduces the C `for(;;)` (`continue`), the `goto SCS_OFFSET_FOUND` (`break 'scs_offset_found`), and the fact that the C loop never exits any other way. `ST_SCS_OFFSET_FOUND` is therefore left unused (harmless: `#![allow(dead_code)]` is set crate-wide).\n2. **No `ST_C10_x` for the OP_ASSERT_SCS `for(;;)` top.** Same E0381 reason for `length`. The two-line loop head (`group_frame_type = GF_NOCAPTURE; RMATCH(Fecode + 1 + LINK_SIZE + length, RM38);`) is inlined at both of its C entry points (end of the setup, and after `length = 0` in `ST_L_RM38`).\n\nFidelity notes: `(GET2(...) << 1) - 2` is done as u32 `wrapping_sub` before widening to `PCRE2_SIZE` (so the C wrap-around for group 0 is preserved); `slot` uses u32 `wrapping_mul` by `name_entry_size`; `_pcre2_OP_lengths_8` is read via `as_ptr().add()` (array is only 173 entries, index is a u8) to avoid any bounds panic; the C `Lextra_size` define (unused in C) is ignored. Note the actual C in this region does **not** use `PRIV(find_bracket)`, `recurse_check`, or `mb->recursive` — the recursion loop check is the frame-walk over `Flast_group_offset`, which is what I translated.\n\n### Verification command output\n\n```\n$ cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-ZSaIwY/translated_rust && cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -E \"^error\" -A15 | head -80\nerror[E0381]: used binding `proptype` is possibly-uninitialized\n   --> src/pcre2_match_ops7.rs:15:16\n    |\n 15 |               if proptype >= 0 {\n    |                  ^^^^^^^^ `proptype` used here but it is possibly-uninitialized\n    |\n   ::: src/pcre2_match_ops6.rs:118:17\n    |\n118 | /                 proptype = {\n119 | |                     let t__ = *(*F).ecode;\n120 | |                     (*F).ecode = (*F).ecode.add(1);\n121 | |                     t__\n122 | |                 } as c_int;\n    | |__________________________- binding initialized here in some conditions\n...\n129 |                   proptype = -1;\n--\nerror: could not compile `pcre2` (lib) due to 1 previous error; 8 warnings emitted\n```\n\nNo error or warning points at `src/pcre2_match_ops10.rs`. To prove my file fully type-checks (resolution errors can abort before typeck), I built a throwaway copy of the crate in `$TMPDIR` with stub helpers and `proptype` initialized: it compiled with **0 errors and 0 warnings**, my file included. The scratch copy has been deleted.\n\n**Heads-up for whoever owns fragments 6/7:** the remaining `E0381` on `proptype` is the same Rust definite-initialization trap — `proptype` is written in `ops6.rs` and read in a different `match` arm in `ops7.rs`. It needs either the write and read in the same arm, or `let mut proptype: c_int = 0;` in the skeleton (`src/pcre2_match.rs:475`).","usage":{"total_tokens":88731,"tool_uses":24,"duration_ms":435195},"uuid":"202d45f6-e95d-4bae-81d3-bb5d5247ba1c","session_id":"69c7dc4d-05f2-4bba-997b-e68ff82cc8bc"}
