RE2_SUBSTITUTE_CASE_NONE`, `PCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_CASE_REVERSE_TITLE_FIRST` (p1, c lines 268-275). I assumed `case_state { to_case: c_int, single_char: BOOL }` and wrote all constant comparisons/assignments as `... as c_int`, which compiles whether p1 types those two file-local constants as `c_int` or `u32`. The callout parameter is typed `pcre2_substitute_case_callout_fn` (the `Option<...>` alias from `internal.rs`), so p3 can pass its local `substitute_case_callout` directly without unwrapping.\n\nC lines 608-741 contain only comments plus the `CHECKMEMCPY` / `CHECKCASECPY_*` / `DELAYEDFORCECASE` macros; they `goto` into `pcre2_substitute()`'s body and Rust loop labels are macro-hygienic, so they must be expanded by hand in p3. I left a comment in the file saying so.\n\n## Verification\n\nWhole-crate build is currently blocked by another agent's half-written file, so first I verified my file in an isolated scratch copy of the crate (`cargo check --release` with a stub for p1's three items and the unparseable file stubbed out): **zero errors and zero warnings pointing at `src/pcre2_substitute_p2.rs`**; the only remaining errors were other agents' placeholders (`compare_opcodes`, `compile_branch`, `parse_regex`, `set_start_bits`, `pso::type_`).\n\nExact output of the required command, run in the project root:\n\n```\n$ cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-ZSaIwY/translated_rust && cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -E \"^error\" -A15 | head -80\nerror: this file contains an unclosed delimiter\n   --> src/pcre2_compile_parse_regex.rs:944:35\n    |\n 78 | ) -> c_int {\n    |            - unclosed delimiter\n...\n122 |     'failed: {\n    |              - unclosed delimiter\n123 |         'failed_forward: {\n    |                          - unclosed delimiter\n124 |             'failed_back: {\n    |                           - unclosed delimiter\n125 |                 'unclosed_parenthesis: {\n    |                                        - unclosed delimi